ses through to the other  side the border is still a real barrier  to these animals meanwhile in Borneo the  local orangutan population has found a  small silver lining to the widespread  destruction of their home cost by  logging these critically endangered Apes  have been observed doing something  unusual instead of traveling through the  forest high up in the tree canopy  they've started walking on the ground  along paths created by loggers one  scientist set up dozens of cameras in  three separate sections of forest each  section representing a different level  of impact from logging in each of the  three areas the cameras recorded photo  of orangutans walking along the timber  paths this is curious because it was  previously thought that orangutans  preferred to stay in the tree canopy to  avoid danger whenever possible experts  suspect that the main reason we see  orangutans opting to walk on the ground  is the significant energy it allows them  to save as opposed to swinging through  the treetops while these Apes may prefer  to save a bit of energy in their daily  lives there is no question that  continued logging is putting this  amazing species at great risk of  extinction in Africa another species of  ape has adapted in an even more striking  way in the rainforests of Guinea many  locals set snares to catch animals to  eat these snares have historically been  the demise of many unfortunate  chimpanzees but now throughout these  rainforests chimpanzees have learned not  only to identify these traps but also to  disarm them even more fascinating is the  fact that these chimps go out of their  way to locate and disarm traps whether  they're doing it to save themselves  trouble or they have some vendetta  against the region's hunters as anyone's  guess the discovery of these chimpanzee  bomb squads was made by accident across  Africa there had been many reports of  chimps being severely injured or killed  by hunting snares until in one region  those reports simply stopped  primatologist observed chimpanzees in  that region of Guinea and saw something  remarkable a group of chimps actively  dismantling a snare the elder of the  group broke the snare then the younger  chimps came forward to inspect it  scientists witnessed similar events in  the region on multiple occasions but so  far the knowledge does not seem to have  spread very far chimpanzees are thought  to learn mostly by trial and error but  in the case of lethal snares and error  likely means death experts believe that  in this case chimpanzees have begun to  learn by observing the effects of the  traps on other animals and slowly  learned which parts of the snare are  dangerous and which can be safely  touched to disarm them it's likely that  over time this new knowledge will spread  to chimpanzee populations across the  continent one final example of the  adaptability and resilience of animals  comes from none other than man's best  friend  specifically the giant population of  stray dogs in Moscow with one stray per  300 humans in Moscow the scientific  community has been able to gather a  wealth of data on these resourceful  animals including dividing the  population into four distinct  personality groups guard dogs wild dogs  scavengers and beggars one scientist has  studied the 35,000 strays for the past  30 years over the decades he's watched  them become  wolf-like losing the spotted coats  wagging tails and trusting friendliness  that domestic dogs exhibit the guard dog  portion of the population hangs around  security personnel across the city  accepting food from them and acting as  semi team deterrence to trespassers the  scavengers wander around the city eating  trash and unattended food the wild dogs  are the most wolf like hunting small  game in the night but the most  interesting group is the beggars these  dogs have adapted some highly  specialized skills they're able to  reliably identify which humans will be  friendly and give them food and th